            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Arabic for a brother-in-law, then the Arabic for a marriage break-up."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: حامل",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "حامل — ḥāmil — \"pregnant\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "زوجة أخي حامل بطفلها الأول. zawjat akhī ḥāmil bi-ṭiflihā al-awwal. My brother's wife is pregnant with her first child."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It has no feminine ending, since only a woman is حامل (ḥāmil)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "حامل (ḥāmil) is built on the native Arabic root ḥ-m-l, \"to carry\" (Lane). Ḥamala, to carry, which you know, is from the same root."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
